// Flying machines and boats: what they cost to build and how they handle.
//
// One item kind ('vehicle') covers all three — same precedent as
// tools/types.js covering a hand spade and a full excavator under one
// 'tool' kind — with a `mode` field ('fly', 'boat' or 'rocket') telling
// vehicles/flight.js, vehicles/boats.js and vehicles/rocket.js which of
// them is allowed to board it, and a `category` used only to group the
// recipe book.
//
// Four flying tiers, each faster and more agile than the last, and each
// needing more of the rarer ores. A hangglider is wood and leaves off the
// surface; a jet fighter needs crystal from the bottom of the world. Four
// boat tiers climb the same wood -> iron -> titanium -> crystal ladder. The
// Cheap Rocket climbs that SAME ladder's top rungs (crystal + titanium)
// rather than needing anything alien — it has to be buildable from a fresh
// home spawn, since it's the only way to ever leave home in the first
// place; see the recipe's own comment below for why an alien-only recipe
// was a real, shipped bug.
//
// The Star Cruiser is the Cheap Rocket's pricier successor: faster and
// tougher, built once you've actually reached another body and dug up its
// STARMETAL (see the recipe's own comment below), and the only craft with a
// hyperspeed drive — see vehicles/rocket.js's own module comment for how
// that mode works. Both ships share `mode: 'rocket'`, so vehicles/rocket.js
// flies either one; only the per-vehicle `hyperspeed`/`hyperspeedSpeed`/
// `hyperspeedTurnRate`/`approachDistance` fields (present on the Star
// Cruiser, absent on the Cheap Rocket) turn the drive on, the same
// precedent the jetpack's own `hover` flag already set for an optional,
// per-vehicle capability read generically by shared flight code.
//
// Handling notes (flying):
//   thrust      — how hard the engine pushes (0 for the unpowered glider)
//   maxSpeed    — blocks per second at full throttle
//   lift        — how much speed converts into staying up
//   sink        — blocks/sec lost when there's no lift keeping you there
//   pitchRate / rollRate / yawRate — radians per second at full input
//   stallSpeed  — below this the nose drops and controls go soft
//
// Handling notes (boats):
//   thrust      — acceleration towards the throttled speed, blocks/sec^2
//   maxSpeed    — blocks per second at full throttle
//   turnRate    — radians per second of rudder at full input
//
// Handling notes (rocket/hyperspeed, on top of the flying notes above):
//   hyperspeed        — has the hyperspeed autopilot-to-target drive at all
//   hyperspeedSpeed   — blocks per second while locked on and cruising
//   hyperspeedTurnRate — how fast the autopilot turns the nose onto target,
//                        radians per second (eased, not instant — see
//                        vehicles/rocket.js)
//   approachDistance  — distance from the TARGET's own surface (not its
//                        centre) at which hyperspeed automatically
//                        disengages for a manual approach

